|> Section
    title=Weak laws of large numbers

    |> Theorem
        label=Weak Law of Large Numbers (WLLN)
        handle=thm-wlln

        If $\mathbf{E}|X_1|<\infty$ then
        $$ \frac{S_n}{n} \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{\mathrm{P}} \mu. $$

    |> Proof
        alt-title=Proof in the case of finite variance

        In the most general case, proving this requires some work -- we shall deduce it from its stronger cousin, the Strong Law of Large Numbers. However, if we assume that $\sigma^2 = \mathbf{V}(X_1)<\infty$, the proof is extremely easy! It suffices to note that in this case $\mathbf{V}(S_n) = n \sigma^2$ (this is true even when the $X_n$'s are not independent but only uncorrelated), or $\mathbf{V}(S_n/n) = \sigma^2/n$.
        From Chebyshev's inequality we then have that for any $\epsilon>0$,
        $$
        \begin{eqnarray*}
        eq:variancebound##<<
        \mathrm{P}(|n^{-1} S_n - \mu|>\epsilon) \le \frac{\sigma^2}{n \epsilon^2} \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{} 0.
        \end{eqnarray*}
        $$
